WITH item AS (SELECT '2026-08-09-camp-mystic-bankruptcy-finances'::text slug,'Texas News'::text category,'Camp Mystic Bankruptcy Filings Reveal Assets, Insurance Pressure and a New Fight Over Flood Lawsuits'::text title,'Court records are giving the first detailed public look at Camp Mystic’s finances as families pursue wrongful-death claims tied to the deadly 2025 Hill Country flood.'::text dek,'San Antonio Express-News'::text source_name,'https://www.expressnews.com/news/article/bankruptcy-court-records-offer-first-look-camp-22378534.php'::text source_url,'2026-08-09T15:34:00Z'::timestamptz published_at,38::int score,TRUE::boolean is_breaking,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/camp-mystic-bankruptcy.svg'::text image_url,'Editorial illustration of a Hill Country camp cabin, courthouse papers and floodwater.'::text image_alt_text,$body$Bankruptcy court records are providing the clearest public look yet at the finances of Camp Mystic and related entities as the long legal aftermath of the deadly 2025 Hill Country flood moves into federal court. The San Antonio Express-News reported that the camp disclosed about $7.9 million in assets and roughly $1.3 million in liabilities, while a related land-holding entity listed substantially more real estate and debt. Those figures are only an early snapshot. They do not yet resolve the much larger question of what resources may ultimately be available to families pursuing wrongful-death claims.

The financial disclosures matter because the camp’s Chapter 11 case is now intertwined with lawsuits filed by families of children and counselors who died in the July 4, 2025 flood. Bankruptcy changes the forum and the sequence in which claims are handled. Instead of every lawsuit proceeding independently toward a judgment, a federal bankruptcy judge can oversee a process that identifies assets, insurance coverage, creditors and potential settlements. Families have argued that accountability for alleged safety failures should not be diluted by the bankruptcy structure, while the camp has said centralized proceedings can preserve limited resources for a broader resolution.

According to the Express-News, Camp Mystic reported about $1.5 million in cash and financial assets as part of its overall asset total. A related entity, Natural Fountains Properties, disclosed roughly $17.5 million in property and about $7.5 million in liabilities. Those numbers immediately raise questions that bankruptcy lawyers and victims’ attorneys will examine closely: which assets legally belong to which entity, what property can be sold or pledged, what insurance policies apply, and whether ownership structures affect the amount available to satisfy claims.

Insurance is another central issue. Camp Mystic has separately sought court protection for members of the Eastland family who face individual lawsuits, arguing that parallel litigation could consume shared insurance coverage and interfere with a coordinated bankruptcy settlement. Families opposing that request argue that nondebtor individuals should not automatically receive the same shield as the company that filed for bankruptcy. The court will have to balance the goal of preserving a common pool of assets against the rights of families to pursue claims against the people they allege were personally responsible for decisions before and during the flood.

The case is emotionally charged because it is not an ordinary business restructuring. The 2025 flood killed children and counselors, and subsequent investigations focused on emergency planning, warnings, evacuation decisions and communications. Any discussion of assets and liabilities therefore sits beside a much larger question of accountability. A balance sheet cannot determine whether someone was negligent. It can, however, determine how a judgment or settlement would be paid, which is why the financial disclosures are important even though they do not answer the underlying factual allegations.

The filings also show how sharply the disaster affected the camp as an operating business. The Express-News reported a dramatic decline in revenue after the flood. That decline is relevant to a Chapter 11 case because reorganization normally depends on the debtor’s ability to generate future income. A business with valuable land but little current operating revenue may have different options than one that can continue producing cash while negotiating with creditors. Whether the camp can or should resume operations is also politically and legally sensitive after state regulators and lawmakers scrutinized its safety plans.

A creditors’ meeting scheduled for later in August will give parties another opportunity to examine the disclosures. In bankruptcy cases, those meetings can be used to ask the debtor about property, debts, financial records and transfers. The numbers may change as claims are filed and assets are valued more precisely. Wrongful-death claims can dwarf ordinary trade debts, and the ultimate size of those claims is not captured by a simple liabilities figure early in the case.

For Texans following the Hill Country flood aftermath, the bankruptcy case will likely become one of the main arenas where legal accountability, compensation and the camp’s future intersect. State investigations and civil lawsuits can establish facts and responsibility, but bankruptcy determines how finite resources are organized when claims exceed what a business can readily pay. That makes transparency especially important. Families need confidence that all relevant assets and insurance are identified, while the court needs accurate information to decide whether any proposed settlement treats claimants fairly.

The next major developments will include rulings on the request to protect individual owners and executives from separate litigation, scrutiny of insurance policies, the creditors’ process and any proposed mediation or reorganization plan. None of those steps will erase the loss behind the case. They will determine whether the legal system handles the competing claims in one coordinated process or allows more of the litigation to proceed separately in state court.

